# L4DEL overlay for the Carleson project. Written from the pinned source.
# Note: some helper files under ToMathlib contain `sorry`; see individual entries.

@file Carleson/Defs.lean
@decl ClassicalCarleson
-- L4DEL: title: Carleson's theorem (statement)
-- L4DEL: intuition: For every continuous 2*pi-periodic complex function f, the partial Fourier sums of f converge to f at almost every point.
-- L4DEL: step: This is only a Prop (a definition of the statement); the proofs are separate theorems.
-- L4DEL: step: The quantifier is over f from the reals to the complex numbers that is continuous and periodic with period 2*pi, and the conclusion is almost-everywhere convergence along N going to infinity.
-- L4DEL: prereq: partialFourierSum
-- L4DEL: see: classical_carleson, classical_carleson_from_carleson_hunt_real
-- L4DEL: term: a.e. = almost everywhere: outside a set of Lebesgue measure zero

@file Carleson/Defs.lean
@decl partialFourierSum
-- L4DEL: title: Partial Fourier sum
-- L4DEL: intuition: The N-th partial sum adds the Fourier modes of f with frequency from -N to N, using coefficients taken over one period [0, 2*pi].
-- L4DEL: step: Sum over integers n from -N to N of the Fourier coefficient of f at n times the n-th exponential character evaluated at x.
-- L4DEL: see: ClassicalCarleson

@file Carleson/Defs.lean
@decl carlesonOperator
-- L4DEL: title: The generalized Carleson operator
-- L4DEL: intuition: It takes, at each point, the largest truncated oscillatory integral of K times f against a phase, over all phases and all inner/outer truncation radii.
-- L4DEL: step: For a fixed phase theta, the linearized operator takes the supremum over radii 0 < R1 < R2 of the norm of the integral of K x y * f y * exp(i theta y) over the annulus R1 < dist(x,y) < R2.
-- L4DEL: step: carlesonOperator is the supremum of this over all phases theta in the space of phases; values lie in the extended nonnegative reals.
-- L4DEL: prereq: linearizedCarlesonOperator, carlesonOperatorIntegrand
-- L4DEL: see: MetricSpaceCarleson
-- L4DEL: term: phase space = the space Theta X of functions used as oscillation phases in the Carleson operator; it carries a compatible family of metrics on balls

@file Carleson/Defs.lean
@decl KernelProofData
-- L4DEL: title: Standing assumptions for the kernel chapters
-- L4DEL: intuition: This class packages everything the early chapters need: a doubling measure, a parameter a with 4 at most a, compatible function distances, and a one-sided Calderon-Zygmund kernel K.
-- L4DEL: step: The fields are d (a doubling measure with doubling constant 2^a), four_le_a, cf (compatible functions), and hcz (K is a one-sided Calderon-Zygmund kernel).
-- L4DEL: step: The docstring notes these are the minimal axioms for the kernel-summand argument and are used before the full ProofData is available.
-- L4DEL: see: MetricSpaceCarleson
-- L4DEL: term: doubling measure = a measure on a metric space for which the measure of a ball of radius 2r is bounded by a fixed constant times the measure of the ball of radius r

@file Carleson/Defs.lean
@decl MetricSpaceCarleson
-- L4DEL: title: Metric space Carleson theorem (statement)
-- L4DEL: intuition: In a doubling metric measure space with a Calderon-Zygmund kernel whose truncated nontangential operator is L2-bounded, the Carleson operator obeys a restricted weak-type estimate integrated over G against F.
-- L4DEL: step: Hypotheses: KernelProofData, an exponent q in (1,2] with conjugate q', measurable sets F and G, a measurable f with norm at most the indicator of F, and an L2 bound for the nontangential operator with constant C_Ts a.
-- L4DEL: step: Conclusion: the integral of carlesonOperator K f over G is at most C1_0_2 a q * vol(G)^(1/q') * vol(F)^(1/q).
-- L4DEL: step: This is a Prop; the proof is metric_carleson in MetricCarleson/Main.lean.
-- L4DEL: prereq: KernelProofData, carlesonOperator, nontangentialOperator, C1_0_2
-- L4DEL: see: metric_carleson, LinearizedMetricCarleson

@file Carleson/MetricCarleson/Main.lean
@decl metric_carleson
-- L4DEL: title: Metric Carleson theorem (Theorem 1.1.1)
-- L4DEL: intuition: The sup over phases is reduced to a countable dense set of phases and rational radii, then to finitely many phases, where the linearized theorem applies, and a monotone limit passes the bound to the supremum.
-- L4DEL: step: Rewrite carlesonOperator as a supremum over a countable dense subset of phases and over rational radii in J102.
-- L4DEL: step: Enumerate the countable phase set and take increasing finite maxima, then use monotone convergence of lintegral to move the supremum outside the integral.
-- L4DEL: step: At each finite stage build a simple-function phase choice Q that realises the maximum, and apply linearized_metric_carleson to it.
-- L4DEL: step: Caveat: the Carleson project has `sorry` in some helper files under ToMathlib (rearrangement, layer cake, Lorentz interpolation); I did not trace which theorems depend on them.
-- L4DEL: prereq: carlesonOperator_eq_biSup_Θ'_J102, linearized_metric_carleson
-- L4DEL: see: MetricSpaceCarleson

@file Carleson/MetricCarleson/Linearized.lean
@decl linearized_metric_carleson
-- L4DEL: title: Linearized metric Carleson theorem (Theorem 1.1.2)
-- L4DEL: intuition: For a fixed phase choice Q, truncate the radii in an increasing family, bound each truncation, and pass to the limit by monotone convergence.
-- L4DEL: step: Write the integral over G of the linearized operator as an increasing supremum over n of the integrals of the n-th truncation lcoConvergent.
-- L4DEL: step: Exchange the integral and the supremum by monotone convergence (lintegral_iSup).
-- L4DEL: step: Bound each truncated integral by the lemma R_truncation, using the L2 bound for the nontangential operators.
-- L4DEL: prereq: lcoConvergent, R_truncation
-- L4DEL: see: LinearizedMetricCarleson, metric_carleson

@file Carleson/Classical/ClassicalCarleson.lean
@decl classical_carleson
-- L4DEL: title: Classical Carleson theorem
-- L4DEL: intuition: Convergence a.e. on one period, together with periodicity, gives convergence a.e. on the whole real line.
-- L4DEL: step: Use periodicity of f and of its partial Fourier sums to reduce a.e. convergence on the line to a.e. convergence on [0, 2*pi].
-- L4DEL: step: The a.e. statement on the interval is carleson_interval', which in turn comes from an exceptional-set estimate (exceptional_set_carleson') through a distribution-function criterion for a.e. convergence.
-- L4DEL: step: Caveat from the file's own comments: this file is an alternative route kept until two_sided_metric_carleson_hasStrongType no longer depends on `sorryAx`. A separate derivation, classical_carleson_from_carleson_hunt_real, goes through the general Carleson-Hunt theorem.
-- L4DEL: prereq: carleson_interval', exceptional_set_carleson'
-- L4DEL: see: ClassicalCarleson, classical_carleson_from_carleson_hunt_real

@file Carleson/Classical/CarlesonHunt.lean
@decl classical_carleson_from_carleson_hunt_real
-- L4DEL: title: Classical Carleson from the Carleson-Hunt theorem
-- L4DEL: intuition: A continuous periodic function is bounded and hence lies in every L^p, so the Carleson-Hunt a.e. convergence result for L^p functions applies.
-- L4DEL: step: Unfold ClassicalCarleson and take f continuous and periodic.
-- L4DEL: step: Apply carleson_hunt_real with p equal to infinity.
-- L4DEL: step: Boundedness of f on [0, 2*pi] from continuity gives membership in L-infinity on the period.
-- L4DEL: prereq: carleson_hunt_real
-- L4DEL: see: ClassicalCarleson, classical_carleson

@file Carleson/TwoSidedCarleson/RestrictedWeakType.lean
@decl two_sided_metric_carleson_hasStrongType
-- L4DEL: title: Strong-type bound for the Carleson operator (Theorem 10.0.1)
-- L4DEL: intuition: Lorentz-type bounds at two exponents bracketing q are combined by real interpolation to give a strong (q,q) bound.
-- L4DEL: step: Choose two exponents q0 = (q+2)/2 and q1 = (q+1)/2 that bracket q.
-- L4DEL: step: Obtain Lorentz-type bounds for the Carleson operator at q0 and q1 from two_sided_metric_carleson_hasLorentzType.
-- L4DEL: step: Apply a real-interpolation theorem for Lorentz spaces (exists_hasLorentzType_real_interpolation) to get HasStrongType with constant C_carleson_hasStrongType.
-- L4DEL: step: Caveat: the file Classical/ClassicalCarleson.lean says this theorem currently depends on `sorryAx`, so it is not fully proved in this version.
-- L4DEL: prereq: C_carleson_hasStrongType, two_sided_metric_carleson_hasLorentzType
-- L4DEL: see: classical_carleson

