@file Carleson/ToMathlib/MeasureTheory/Integral/Layercake.lean
@decl MeasureTheory.lintegral_comp_eq_lintegral_distribution_mul
-- L4DEL: title: The layer cake formula / Cavalieri's principle / tail probability formula: Let `f` be a non-negative measurable function on a measure space
-- L4DEL: intuition: The layer cake formula / **Cavalieri's principle** / tail probability formula: Let `f` be a non-negative measurable function on a measure space. Let `G` be an increasing absolutely continuous function on the positive real line, vanishing at the origin, with derivative `G' = g`. Then the integral of the composition `G ∘ f` can be written as the integral over the positive real line of the "tail measures" `μ {ω | f(ω) > t}` of `f` weighted by `g`. Roughly speaking, the statement is: `∫⁻ (G ∘ f) ∂μ = ∫⁻ t in 0..∞, g(t) * μ {ω | f(ω) > t}`. See `MeasureTheory.lintegral_comp_eq_lintegral_meas_le_mul` for a version with sets of the form `{ω | f(ω) ≥ t}` instead.

@file Carleson/ToMathlib/MeasureTheory/Integral/Layercake.lean
@decl MeasureTheory.lintegral_comp_eq_lintegral_meas_le_mul'
-- L4DEL: title: The layer cake formula / Cavalieri's principle / tail probability formula: Let `f` be a non-negative measurable function on a measure space
-- L4DEL: intuition: The layer cake formula / Cavalieri's principle / tail probability formula: Let `f` be a non-negative measurable function on a measure space. Let `G` be an increasing absolutely continuous function on the positive real line, vanishing at the origin, with derivative `G' = g`. Then the integral of the composition `G ∘ f` can be written as the integral over the positive real line of the "tail measures" `μ {ω | f(ω) ≥ t}` of `f` weighted by `g`. Roughly speaking, the statement is: `∫⁻ (G ∘ f) ∂μ = ∫⁻ t in 0..∞, g(t) * μ {ω | f(ω) ≥ t}`. See `lintegral_comp_eq_lintegral_distribution_mul` for a version with sets of the form `{ω | f(ω) > t}` instead.

@file Carleson/ToMathlib/MeasureTheory/Integral/Layercake.lean
@decl MeasureTheory.lintegral_eq_lintegral_distribution
-- L4DEL: title: Documented declaration: lintegral_eq_lintegral_distribution
-- L4DEL: intuition: The standard case of the layer cake formula / Cavalieri's principle / tail probability formula: For a nonnegative function `f` on a measure space, the Lebesgue integral of `f` can be written (roughly speaking) as: `∫⁻ f ∂μ = ∫⁻ t in 0..∞, μ {ω | f(ω) ≥ t}`. See `MeasureTheory.lintegral_eq_lintegral_meas_lt` for a version with sets of the form `{ω | f(ω) > t}` instead.

@file Carleson/ToMathlib/MeasureTheory/Integral/Layercake.lean
@decl MeasureTheory.lintegral_eq_lintegral_meas_le'
-- L4DEL: title: Documented declaration: lintegral_eq_lintegral_meas_le'
-- L4DEL: intuition: The standard case of the layer cake formula / Cavalieri's principle / tail probability formula: For a nonnegative function `f` on a measure space, the Lebesgue integral of `f` can be written (roughly speaking) as: `∫⁻ f ∂μ = ∫⁻ t in 0..∞, μ {ω | f(ω) > t}`. See `lintegral_eq_lintegral_meas_le` for a version with sets of the form `{ω | f(ω) ≥ t}` instead.

@file Carleson/ToMathlib/MeasureTheory/Integral/MeanInequalities.lean
@decl ENNReal.convolution_zero_of_c_nonpos
-- L4DEL: title: Used to handle trivial case `c ≤ 0` when proving versions of Young's convolution inequality assuming `∀ (x y : G), ‖L (f x) (g y)‖ ≤ c * ‖f x‖ * ‖g y‖)`
-- L4DEL: intuition: Used to handle trivial case `c ≤ 0` when proving versions of Young's convolution inequality assuming `∀ (x y : G), ‖L (f x) (g y)‖ ≤ c * ‖f x‖ * ‖g y‖)`

@file Carleson/ToMathlib/MeasureTheory/Integral/MeanInequalities.lean
@decl ENNReal.eLpNorm_convolution_le_enorm_mul
-- L4DEL: title: Documented declaration: eLpNorm_convolution_le_enorm_mul
-- L4DEL: intuition: **Young's convolution inequality**: the `L^r` seminorm of a convolution `(f ⋆[L, μ] g)` is bounded by `‖L‖ₑ` times the product of the `L^p` and `L^q` seminorms, where `1 / p + 1 / q = 1 / r + 1`.

@file Carleson/ToMathlib/MeasureTheory/Integral/MeanInequalities.lean
@decl ENNReal.eLpNorm_convolution_le_enorm_mul'
-- L4DEL: title: Documented declaration: eLpNorm_convolution_le_enorm_mul'
-- L4DEL: intuition: **Young's convolution inequality**: the `L^r` seminorm of a convolution `(f ⋆[L, μ] g)` is bounded by `‖L‖ₑ` times the product of the `L^p` and `L^q` seminorms, where `1 / p + 1 / q = 1 / r + 1`.

@file Carleson/ToMathlib/MeasureTheory/Integral/MeanInequalities.lean
@decl ENNReal.eLpNorm_convolution_le_of_norm_le_mul
-- L4DEL: title: Documented declaration: eLpNorm_convolution_le_of_norm_le_mul
-- L4DEL: intuition: **Young's convolution inequality**: the `L^r` seminorm of a convolution `(f ⋆[L, μ] g)` is bounded by `‖L‖ₑ` times the product of the `L^p` and `L^q` seminorms, where `1 / p + 1 / q = 1 / r + 1`. Here `‖L‖ₑ` is replaced with a bound for `L` restricted to the ranges of `f` and `g`; see `eLpNorm_convolution_le_enorm_mul` for a version using `‖L‖ₑ` explicitly.

@file Carleson/ToMathlib/MeasureTheory/Integral/MeanInequalities.lean
@decl ENNReal.eLpNorm_convolution_le_of_norm_le_mul'
-- L4DEL: title: Documented declaration: eLpNorm_convolution_le_of_norm_le_mul'
-- L4DEL: intuition: **Young's convolution inequality**: the `L^r` seminorm of a convolution `(f ⋆[L, μ] g)` is bounded by `‖L‖ₑ` times the product of the `L^p` and `L^q` seminorms, where `1 / p + 1 / q = 1 / r + 1`. Here `‖L‖ₑ` is replaced with a bound for `L` restricted to the ranges of `f` and `g`; see `eLpNorm_convolution_le_enorm_mul` for a version using `‖L‖ₑ` explicitly.

@file Carleson/ToMathlib/MeasureTheory/Integral/MeanInequalities.lean
@decl ENNReal.eLpNorm_convolution_le_of_norm_le_mul_aux
-- L4DEL: title: Auxiliary result to prove the following versions with simpler assumptions on `f` and `g`
-- L4DEL: intuition: Auxiliary result to prove the following versions with simpler assumptions on `f` and `g`

@file Carleson/ToMathlib/MeasureTheory/Integral/MeanInequalities.lean
@decl ENNReal.eLpNorm_convolution_le_ofReal_aux
-- L4DEL: title: Auxiliary inequality used to prove versions with simpler conditions on `f` and `g`
-- L4DEL: intuition: Auxiliary inequality used to prove versions with simpler conditions on `f` and `g`

@file Carleson/ToMathlib/MeasureTheory/Integral/MeanInequalities.lean
@decl ENNReal.eLpNorm_Ioc_convolution_le_enorm_mul
-- L4DEL: title: Documented declaration: eLpNorm_Ioc_convolution_le_enorm_mul
-- L4DEL: intuition: **Young's convolution inequality** on (a, a + T]: the `L^r` seminorm of the convolution of `T`-periodic functions over (a, a + T] is bounded by `‖L‖ₑ` times the product of the `L^p` and `L^q` seminorms on that interval, where `1 / p + 1 / q = 1 / r + 1`.

@file Carleson/ToMathlib/MeasureTheory/Integral/MeanInequalities.lean
@decl ENNReal.eLpNorm_Ioc_convolution_le_of_norm_le_mul
-- L4DEL: title: Documented declaration: eLpNorm_Ioc_convolution_le_of_norm_le_mul
-- L4DEL: intuition: **Young's convolution inequality** on (a, a + T]: the `L^r` seminorm of the convolution of `T`-periodic functions over (a, a + T] is bounded by `‖L‖ₑ` times the product of the `L^p` and `L^q` seminorms on that interval, where `1 / p + 1 / q = 1 / r + 1`. Here `‖L‖ₑ` is replaced with a bound for `L` restricted to the ranges of `f` and `g`; see `eLpNorm_Ioc_convolution_le_enorm_mul` for a version using `‖L‖ₑ` explicitly.

@file Carleson/ToMathlib/MeasureTheory/Integral/MeanInequalities.lean
